<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kashmir Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1339 CE – 1561 CE · capital Srinagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Muslim sultanate of the Kashmir valley founded by Shah Mir, under which the region became predominantly Muslim.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-mir">Shah Mir</a>, a migrant from the north-west, rose to prominence at the court of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. After the death of Queen Kota <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rani">Rani</a>, he seized the throne in 1339, taking the title Shams al-Din, and founded the Shah Mir dynasty at Srinagar.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miri">Miris</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread widely, encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-sayyid-ali-hamadani">Mir Sayyid Ali Hamadani</a>, who brought Persian crafts including shawl-weaving and papier-mache. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a> is remembered for destroying temples, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zain-ul-abidin">Zain al-Abidin</a>, known as Budshah, the great king, restored tolerance, recalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmiri-pandits">Pandits</a>, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works translated into Persian and built canals and bridges.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zain-ul-abidin">Zain al-Abidin</a> the dynasty declined amid noble factions. The Mughal commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-muhammad-haidar-dughlat">Mirza Haidar Dughlat</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from 1540 to 1551, and in 1561 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chak-dynasty">Chak dynasty</a> took the throne, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-mir">Shah Mir</a> rule. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> annexed Kashmir in 1586.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kashmir-sultanate.jpg" alt="Kashmir Sultanate" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: RootOfAllLight · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blood_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1339 CE – 1561 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Srinagar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shah Mir, Sikandar Butshikan, Zain al-Abidin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Kashmiri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shah Mir dynasty, Sultanate of Kashmir, Shahmiri dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1450&amp;focus=kashmir-sultanate" aria-label="Kashmir Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6585 942 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kashmir Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6905 1198L6905 1198M6905 1198L6905 1198"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6905 1198h0M6905 1198h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6905 1198h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6905 1198h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Srinagar. <a href="/atlas?year=1450&amp;focus=kashmir-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
